IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `safw-dates`

-- Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) -- THE RULES, seat-neutral, for the provisions a person bringing an employment claim meets:
-- who is an employee and when the Act applies (ss 4, 5, 6); monetary claims in the South Australian Employment Court
-- (ss 9, 10, 30-36); the right to apply to SAET (s 17(2)); severance on redundancy (s 72B(5)-(8)); an outworker's
-- unpaid remuneration claim (ss 99B, 99D-99G); civil penalties for unpaid entitlements (ss 104-104C); unfair dismissal
-- (ss 105-111, Schedule 8); dismissal or prejudice for a prohibited reason or for taking part in industrial proceedings
-- (ss 115, 116B, 223, 233, 235(2)); the indexation of the Act's (indexed) amounts (s 4(4)).
--
-- Answers are named outcomes. Where the text says no in its own words ("means", "if", "only if", "may only", "must
-- not", a period the Act fixes) the outcome is a "no: ..." naming the provision; where the text is silent or a list is
-- not closed, it says "not covered". Judgments the Act gives to SAET, the Court or a person ("satisfied", "considers",
-- "good reason", "reasonable grounds", "cogent reasons") are inputs, never decided here. No REFUSE.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website (version of 22 August 2026, fetched 6 November
-- 2026). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) -- the claims provisions`

§§ `Time limits as regulative rules`

-- Each time limit is a duty with a deadline, counted in days from the event that starts it (day 0). A step taken on
-- the last day keeps the rule; a later step comes after the LEST BREACH.

DECLARE `Claim party` IS ONE OF `I, the claimant`, `the employer`, `the prosecutor`

DECLARE `Claim step` IS ONE OF
    `make the monetary claim (s 31)`
    `apply for a severance payment order (s 72B(6)(b))`
    `serve the unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(3))`
    `commence civil penalty proceedings (s 104A(9))`
    `apply to SAET for unfair dismissal relief (s 106(1))`
    `apply for costs (s 110(3))`
    `commence the prosecution (s 235(2))`

GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`, `the party` IS A `Claim party`, `the step` IS A `Claim step`, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` `the rule` `the party` `the step` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the party` `the step` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 31 -- a monetary claim must be made within 6 years after the sum claimed became payable` `days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the claimant`
    MUST `make the monetary claim (s 31)`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the claimant` BECAUSE "no monetary claim within 6 years after the sum became payable (s 31)"

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 72B(6)(b) -- the application must be made within 21 days after the notice or the termination` `days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the claimant`
    MUST `apply for a severance payment order (s 72B(6)(b))`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the claimant` BECAUSE "no application within 21 days after the notice of redundancy was given or the employment was terminated (s 72B(6)(b))"

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 99D(3) -- the unpaid remuneration claim must be made within 6 months after the work is completed` `days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the claimant`
    MUST `serve the unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(3))`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the claimant` BECAUSE "no unpaid remuneration claim within 6 months after the work was completed (s 99D(3))"

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 104A(9) -- civil penalty proceedings may be commenced within 6 years after the contravention` `days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the claimant`
    MUST `commence civil penalty proceedings (s 104A(9))`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the claimant` BECAUSE "no civil penalty proceedings within 6 years after the day the contravention occurred (s 104A(9))"

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 106(1) -- the employee applies before the end of 21 days from the date the dismissal takes effect` `days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the claimant`
    MUST `apply to SAET for unfair dismissal relief (s 106(1))`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the claimant` BECAUSE "no application before the end of 21 days from the date the dismissal took effect (s 106(1))"

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 110(3) -- an application for costs must be made within 14 days after the determination or discontinuance` `days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST `apply for costs (s 110(3))`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "no application for costs within 14 days after the determination or discontinuance (s 110(3))"

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim step`
`s 235(2) -- a prosecution must be commenced within 2 years after the offence` `days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the prosecutor`
    MUST `commence the prosecution (s 235(2))`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the prosecutor` BECAUSE "no prosecution within 2 years after the date the offence is alleged to have been committed (s 235(2))"

§§ `s 4(4): (indexed) amounts`

-- s 4(4): an amount followed by "(indexed)" is adjusted on 1 January each year by multiplying it by State average
-- full-time adult total earnings (seasonally adjusted) at 30 June of the previous year, divided by the same figure at
-- 30 June of the year Parliament fixed the amount. The earnings figures are published statistics, not deposited.
GIVEN `the stated amount` IS A NUMBER, `earnings at 30 June last year` IS A NUMBER, `earnings at 30 June of the year fixed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw the indexed amount (s 4(4))` `the stated amount` `earnings at 30 June last year` `earnings at 30 June of the year fixed` MEANS
    `the stated amount` TIMES (`earnings at 30 June last year` DIVIDED BY `earnings at 30 June of the year fixed`)

-- The amounts as the Act states them.
`safw s 105A(1)(a) stated amount` MEANS 100322
`safw s 109(3) stated amount` MEANS 33100

§§ `Chapter 1: employee, contract of employment, outworker, application of the Act (ss 4, 5, 6)`

-- s 5(1)-(3) outworker ("A person is an outworker if"). Engaged "for the purposes of the trade or business of
-- another" personally, or through a body corporate for which the person personally performs all or a substantial part
-- of its work.
GIVEN `engaged for another's trade or business` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `to work on, process, clean or pack articles or materials, or do clerical work` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `in or about a private residence or other non-business premises` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `to arrange, distribute or collect outworkers' work` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `engaged to clean a third person's private residence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `safw is an outworker (s 5(1)-(3))` `engaged for another's trade or business` `to work on, process, clean or pack articles or materials, or do clerical work` `in or about a private residence or other non-business premises` `to arrange, distribute or collect outworkers' work` `engaged to clean a third person's private residence` IF
        `engaged for another's trade or business`
    AND (NOT `engaged to clean a third person's private residence`)
    AND (    (`to work on, process, clean or pack articles or materials, or do clerical work` AND `in or about a private residence or other non-business premises`)
         OR `to arrange, distribute or collect outworkers' work`)

DECLARE `Engagement` IS ONE OF
    `a contract recognised at common law as a contract of employment, for remuneration in an industry`
    `an engagement to drive a vehicle not registered in my name to provide a public passenger service`
    `an engagement to carry out personally the work of cleaning premises`
    `an engagement to carry out work as an outworker`
    `some other engagement`

-- s 4(1) "contract of employment means" (a)-(d), with the taxi exception to (b).
GIVEN `the engagement` IS AN `Engagement`, `the vehicle is a taxi` IS A BOOLEAN, `an outworker` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `the engagement` `the vehicle is a taxi` `an outworker` IF
    CONSIDER `the engagement`
    WHEN `a contract recognised at common law as a contract of employment, for remuneration in an industry` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an engagement to drive a vehicle not registered in my name to provide a public passenger service` THEN (NOT `the vehicle is a taxi`)
    WHEN `an engagement to carry out personally the work of cleaning premises` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an engagement to carry out work as an outworker` THEN `an outworker`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Application answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are an employee under this Act (s 4(1)), and s 6 does not take your employment out of the Act`
    `yes, but only in relation to an excluded subject matter: your employer is a national system employer (s 6(aa))`
    `no: you are not employed under a contract of employment as s 4(1) defines it, and you are not a public employee, so you are not an employee under this Act`
    `no: you are an outworker, and outside Chapter 1 the Act applies to outworkers only if an award or enterprise agreement provision relates to outworkers or a regulation extends the Act to them (s 5(4))`
    `no: you are employed by your spouse, domestic partner or parent (s 6(a))`
    `no: the regulations exclude your employment from the Act (s 6(b))`
    `no: your employer is a national system employer under the Commonwealth Fair Work Act 2009, and the matter is not an excluded subject matter (s 6(aa))`

GIVEN `contract of employment` IS A BOOLEAN, `public employee` IS A BOOLEAN, `an outworker` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `an award, agreement or regulation brings outworkers in` IS A BOOLEAN, `employed by spouse, domestic partner or parent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `excluded by regulation` IS A BOOLEAN, `national system employer` IS A BOOLEAN, `excluded subject matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Application answer`
`safw does the Act apply to my employment (ss 4, 5(4), 6)` `contract of employment` `public employee` `an outworker` `an award, agreement or regulation brings outworkers in` `employed by spouse, domestic partner or parent` `excluded by regulation` `national system employer` `excluded subject matter` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`contract of employment` OR `public employee`)) THEN `no: you are not employed under a contract of employment as s 4(1) defines it, and you are not a public employee, so you are not an employee under this Act`
           IF `an outworker` AND (NOT `an award, agreement or regulation brings outworkers in`) THEN `no: you are an outworker, and outside Chapter 1 the Act applies to outworkers only if an award or enterprise agreement provision relates to outworkers or a regulation extends the Act to them (s 5(4))`
           IF `employed by spouse, domestic partner or parent` THEN `no: you are employed by your spouse, domestic partner or parent (s 6(a))`
           IF `excluded by regulation` THEN `no: the regulations exclude your employment from the Act (s 6(b))`
           IF `national system employer` AND `excluded subject matter` THEN `yes, but only in relation to an excluded subject matter: your employer is a national system employer (s 6(aa))`
           IF `national system employer` THEN `no: your employer is a national system employer under the Commonwealth Fair Work Act 2009, and the matter is not an excluded subject matter (s 6(aa))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: you are an employee under this Act (s 4(1)), and s 6 does not take your employment out of the Act`

§§ `Chapter 2 Part 5: monetary claims (ss 9, 10, 30-36)`

DECLARE `Claim kind` IS ONE OF
    `a sum due to me from my employer or former employer under this Act, an award, an enterprise agreement or my contract of employment`
    `a sum due to me from my employer or former employer under a workplace law`
    `compensation from my employer or former employer for failing to make superannuation contributions for my benefit`
    `payment of a benefit from the trustee of a superannuation fund to which contributions have been made`
    `a liquidated sum or debt under my common law contract of employment`
    `damages for breach of my common law contract of employment`
    `some other claim`

-- s 30: "monetary claim means a claim under section 9 or a claim for a sum or a debt under section 10".
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Claim kind`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `safw is a monetary claim (s 30)` `the kind` IF
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `damages for breach of my common law contract of employment` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `some other claim` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

DECLARE `Monetary claim answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim under s 9(1)(a)`
    `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim under s 9(1)(c), and may direct that the compensation be paid into a superannuation fund for you (s 35(2))`
    `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim against the trustee under s 9(1)(d)`
    `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim for a sum or debt under s 10(1)(b)`
    `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear a claim for damages for breach of the contract under s 10(1)(a); it is not a monetary claim under s 30, so ss 31, 33 and 36 do not apply to it`
    `no: the workplace law gives a court jurisdiction over this claim, so it cannot be made under s 9(1); bring it under that law (s 9(2))`
    `not covered: a claim under a workplace law is in s 9(1) only for conduct after 1 January 2026 (Fair Work (Worker Entitlements) Amendment Act 2025, Sch 1 cl 4); the earlier text is not deposited`
    `not now: proceedings based on the same claim have begun in another court and have not been withdrawn or struck out, so the Court may not hear it (s 33)`
    `not covered: the claim is not one of the kinds in s 9(1) or s 10(1)`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Claim kind`, `the workplace law gives a court jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN, `conduct after 1 January 2026` IS A BOOLEAN, `same claim begun in another court and still on foot` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Monetary claim answer`
`safw can the Court hear my claim (ss 9, 10, 33)` `the kind` `the workplace law gives a court jurisdiction` `conduct after 1 January 2026` `same claim begun in another court and still on foot` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `some other claim` THEN `not covered: the claim is not one of the kinds in s 9(1) or s 10(1)`
    WHEN `damages for breach of my common law contract of employment` THEN `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear a claim for damages for breach of the contract under s 10(1)(a); it is not a monetary claim under s 30, so ss 31, 33 and 36 do not apply to it`
    WHEN `a sum due to me from my employer or former employer under a workplace law` THEN
        BRANCH IF `the workplace law gives a court jurisdiction` THEN `no: the workplace law gives a court jurisdiction over this claim, so it cannot be made under s 9(1); bring it under that law (s 9(2))`
               IF (NOT `conduct after 1 January 2026`) THEN `not covered: a claim under a workplace law is in s 9(1) only for conduct after 1 January 2026 (Fair Work (Worker Entitlements) Amendment Act 2025, Sch 1 cl 4); the earlier text is not deposited`
               IF `same claim begun in another court and still on foot` THEN `not now: proceedings based on the same claim have begun in another court and have not been withdrawn or struck out, so the Court may not hear it (s 33)`
               OTHERWISE `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim under s 9(1)(a)`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `same claim begun in another court and still on foot`
        THEN `not now: proceedings based on the same claim have begun in another court and have not been withdrawn or struck out, so the Court may not hear it (s 33)`
        ELSE `safw the head of jurisdiction` `the kind`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Claim kind`
GIVETH A `Monetary claim answer`
`safw the head of jurisdiction` `the kind` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `compensation from my employer or former employer for failing to make superannuation contributions for my benefit` THEN `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim under s 9(1)(c), and may direct that the compensation be paid into a superannuation fund for you (s 35(2))`
    WHEN `payment of a benefit from the trustee of a superannuation fund to which contributions have been made` THEN `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim against the trustee under s 9(1)(d)`
    WHEN `a liquidated sum or debt under my common law contract of employment` THEN `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim for a sum or debt under s 10(1)(b)`
    OTHERWISE `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim under s 9(1)(a)`

-- s 31.
GIVEN `the sum became payable on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`safw the last day for a monetary claim (s 31)` `the sum became payable on` MEANS `safw the last day of` 6 `years after` `the sum became payable on`

DECLARE `Limitation answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: the claim was made within 6 years after the sum became payable (s 31)`
    `out of time: more than 6 years have passed since the sum became payable (s 31)`
    `no time limit: none applies to a claim for the non-payment of superannuation contributions (s 31)`
    `too early: the sum had not become payable when the claim was made`
    `s 31 does not apply: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Claim kind`, `the sum became payable on` IS A DATE, `the claim was made on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Limitation answer`
`safw is my monetary claim in time (s 31)` `the kind` `the sum became payable on` `the claim was made on` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `safw is a monetary claim (s 30)` `the kind`) THEN `s 31 does not apply: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`
           IF `the kind` EQUALS `compensation from my employer or former employer for failing to make superannuation contributions for my benefit` THEN `no time limit: none applies to a claim for the non-payment of superannuation contributions (s 31)`
           IF `safw the day` `the claim was made on` `is before` `the sum became payable on` THEN `too early: the sum had not become payable when the claim was made`
           IF `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 31 -- a monetary claim must be made within 6 years after the sum claimed became payable` (`safw days from` `the sum became payable on` `to` (`safw the last day for a monetary claim (s 31)` `the sum became payable on`))) `I, the claimant` `make the monetary claim (s 31)` (`safw days from` `the sum became payable on` `to` `the claim was made on`) THEN `in time: the claim was made within 6 years after the sum became payable (s 31)`
           OTHERWISE `out of time: more than 6 years have passed since the sum became payable (s 31)`

-- s 32.
DECLARE `Claimant position` IS ONE OF
    `I am the claimant, and an adult`
    `I am the claimant, and under 18`
    `an association making the claim on the claimant's behalf`
    `the personal representative of the claimant, who has died`
    `a beneficiary of the estate of the claimant, who has died`
    `someone else making the claim`

DECLARE `Who may claim answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the employee or former employee to whom the sum is due may make the claim (s 9(1), s 17(1)(c))`
    `yes: a minor may make a monetary claim as if the minor had attained the age of majority (s 32(2))`
    `yes: an association may make a monetary claim on the claimant's behalf (s 32(1))`
    `yes: a claim for money that should have been paid to or for a person who is now dead may be made by the personal representative (s 32(3))`
    `yes: a claim for money that should have been paid to or for a person who is now dead may be made by a beneficiary of the estate (s 32(3))`
    `not covered: s 32 does not name you as someone who may make the claim`

GIVEN `the position` IS A `Claimant position`
GIVETH A `Who may claim answer`
`safw who may make the monetary claim (s 32)` `the position` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the position`
    WHEN `I am the claimant, and an adult` THEN `yes: the employee or former employee to whom the sum is due may make the claim (s 9(1), s 17(1)(c))`
    WHEN `I am the claimant, and under 18` THEN `yes: a minor may make a monetary claim as if the minor had attained the age of majority (s 32(2))`
    WHEN `an association making the claim on the claimant's behalf` THEN `yes: an association may make a monetary claim on the claimant's behalf (s 32(1))`
    WHEN `the personal representative of the claimant, who has died` THEN `yes: a claim for money that should have been paid to or for a person who is now dead may be made by the personal representative (s 32(3))`
    WHEN `a beneficiary of the estate of the claimant, who has died` THEN `yes: a claim for money that should have been paid to or for a person who is now dead may be made by a beneficiary of the estate (s 32(3))`
    OTHERWISE `not covered: s 32 does not name you as someone who may make the claim`

-- s 34.
DECLARE `Interest answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the judgment must include interest, or a lump sum instead of interest, taking into account the period from the day the cause of action arose to the day of judgment; there is no interest on interest (s 34(1), (1a), (2)(a))`
    `no: the Court includes interest only on the application of the party in whose favour the judgment is made (s 34(1))`
    `no: the Court has found good reason not to include interest (s 34(1))`
    `no: the judgment was given by consent, and the parties have not consented to interest (s 34(2)(c))`

GIVEN `judgment by consent` IS A BOOLEAN, `the parties consent to interest` IS A BOOLEAN, `I applied for interest` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Court finds good reason not to` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Interest answer`
`safw will my judgment include interest (s 34)` `judgment by consent` `the parties consent to interest` `I applied for interest` `the Court finds good reason not to` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `judgment by consent` AND (NOT `the parties consent to interest`) THEN `no: the judgment was given by consent, and the parties have not consented to interest (s 34(2)(c))`
           IF (NOT `I applied for interest`) THEN `no: the Court includes interest only on the application of the party in whose favour the judgment is made (s 34(1))`
           IF `the Court finds good reason not to` THEN `no: the Court has found good reason not to include interest (s 34(1))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the judgment must include interest, or a lump sum instead of interest, taking into account the period from the day the cause of action arose to the day of judgment; there is no interest on interest (s 34(1), (1a), (2)(a))`

GIVEN `the cause of action arose on` IS A DATE, `judgment delivered on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw the days interest must take into account (s 34(1a))` `the cause of action arose on` `judgment delivered on` MEANS
    `safw days from` `the cause of action arose on` `to` `judgment delivered on`

-- s 36 (and s 104C, which is worded as s 36(2)).
GIVEN `vexatiously or without reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN, `unreasonable act or omission caused the costs` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `safw a ground for costs (s 36(2), s 104C)` `vexatiously or without reasonable cause` `unreasonable act or omission caused the costs` IF
    `vexatiously or without reasonable cause` OR `unreasonable act or omission caused the costs`

DECLARE `Costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 36 does not apply to a claim under s 10: costs are awarded as in a corresponding claim in the District Court or the Magistrates Court (s 36(3), s 10(6))`
    `not covered: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`
    `no: at first instance the Court may award costs on a monetary claim only to cover a claimant's reasonable expenses of establishing the present value of lost superannuation, never against a claimant (s 36(1))`
    `no: at first instance the Court may award costs on a monetary claim only to cover a claimant's reasonable expenses of establishing the present value of lost superannuation (s 36(1)(a))`
    `possibly: the Court may award you costs to cover the reasonable expenses you incurred to establish the present value of the loss (s 36(1)(a))`
    `possibly: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs, because the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(1)(b), (2))`
    `no: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs only if the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(2))`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Claim kind`, `on appeal` IS A BOOLEAN, `I acted vexatiously or without reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN, `my unreasonable act or omission caused the costs` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`safw can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim (s 36)` `the kind` `on appeal` `I acted vexatiously or without reasonable cause` `my unreasonable act or omission caused the costs` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the kind` EQUALS `a liquidated sum or debt under my common law contract of employment` OR `the kind` EQUALS `damages for breach of my common law contract of employment` THEN `s 36 does not apply to a claim under s 10: costs are awarded as in a corresponding claim in the District Court or the Magistrates Court (s 36(3), s 10(6))`
           IF (NOT `safw is a monetary claim (s 30)` `the kind`) THEN `not covered: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`
           IF (NOT `on appeal`) THEN `no: at first instance the Court may award costs on a monetary claim only to cover a claimant's reasonable expenses of establishing the present value of lost superannuation, never against a claimant (s 36(1))`
           IF `safw a ground for costs (s 36(2), s 104C)` `I acted vexatiously or without reasonable cause` `my unreasonable act or omission caused the costs` THEN `possibly: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs, because the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(1)(b), (2))`
           OTHERWISE `no: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs only if the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(2))`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Claim kind`, `on appeal` IS A BOOLEAN, `they acted vexatiously or without reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN, `their unreasonable act or omission caused my costs` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`safw can I recover my costs of my monetary claim (s 36)` `the kind` `on appeal` `they acted vexatiously or without reasonable cause` `their unreasonable act or omission caused my costs` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the kind` EQUALS `a liquidated sum or debt under my common law contract of employment` OR `the kind` EQUALS `damages for breach of my common law contract of employment` THEN `s 36 does not apply to a claim under s 10: costs are awarded as in a corresponding claim in the District Court or the Magistrates Court (s 36(3), s 10(6))`
           IF (NOT `safw is a monetary claim (s 30)` `the kind`) THEN `not covered: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`
           IF `the kind` EQUALS `compensation from my employer or former employer for failing to make superannuation contributions for my benefit` THEN `possibly: the Court may award you costs to cover the reasonable expenses you incurred to establish the present value of the loss (s 36(1)(a))`
           IF (NOT `on appeal`) THEN `no: at first instance the Court may award costs on a monetary claim only to cover a claimant's reasonable expenses of establishing the present value of lost superannuation (s 36(1)(a))`
           IF `safw a ground for costs (s 36(2), s 104C)` `they acted vexatiously or without reasonable cause` `their unreasonable act or omission caused my costs` THEN `possibly: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs, because the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(1)(b), (2))`
           OTHERWISE `no: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs only if the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(2))`

-- s 35(1): instalments. A discretion of the Court; the answer says what it may do.
DECLARE `Instalments answer` IS ONE OF
    `the Court may authorise or direct payment by instalments (s 35(1)), and may direct that compensation for unpaid superannuation contributions be paid to a superannuation fund for you (s 35(2))`
    `the Court may authorise or direct payment by instalments (s 35(1))`
    `not covered: s 35 applies to claims under Chapter 2 Part 1 for a sum due or other monetary amount (s 35(3))`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Claim kind`
GIVETH AN `Instalments answer`
`safw how may a monetary judgment be paid (s 35)` `the kind` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `compensation from my employer or former employer for failing to make superannuation contributions for my benefit` THEN `the Court may authorise or direct payment by instalments (s 35(1)), and may direct that compensation for unpaid superannuation contributions be paid to a superannuation fund for you (s 35(2))`
    WHEN `some other claim` THEN `not covered: s 35 applies to claims under Chapter 2 Part 1 for a sum due or other monetary amount (s 35(3))`
    OTHERWISE `the Court may authorise or direct payment by instalments (s 35(1))`

§§ `s 17(2): bringing an application to SAET`

DECLARE `As of right answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may bring the application as of right, because another provision of this Act authorises it (s 17(2))`
    `yes: you may bring the application, because SAET is satisfied that the claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance and that no other impartial grievance resolution process is or has been reasonably available to you (s 17(2)(a), (b))`
    `no: unless another provision of this Act authorises the application, you must satisfy SAET both that the claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance and that no other impartial grievance resolution process is or has been reasonably available to you (s 17(2))`

GIVEN `another provision authorises the application` IS A BOOLEAN, `SAET is satisfied of a genuine industrial grievance` IS A BOOLEAN, `SAET is satisfied no other impartial process is or was reasonably available` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `As of right answer`
`safw can I bring the application to SAET (s 17(2))` `another provision authorises the application` `SAET is satisfied of a genuine industrial grievance` `SAET is satisfied no other impartial process is or was reasonably available` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `another provision authorises the application` THEN `yes: you may bring the application as of right, because another provision of this Act authorises it (s 17(2))`
           IF `SAET is satisfied of a genuine industrial grievance` AND `SAET is satisfied no other impartial process is or was reasonably available` THEN `yes: you may bring the application, because SAET is satisfied that the claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance and that no other impartial grievance resolution process is or has been reasonably available to you (s 17(2)(a), (b))`
           OTHERWISE `no: unless another provision of this Act authorises the application, you must satisfy SAET both that the claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance and that no other impartial grievance resolution process is or has been reasonably available to you (s 17(2))`

§§ `s 72B(5)-(8): severance on redundancy`

DECLARE `Severance applicant` IS ONE OF
    `I, the employee, alone or as one of a group of employees`
    `a registered association acting for me or my group`
    `someone else applying`

DECLARE `Severance answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, in time: you applied within 21 days after you were given notice of the pending redundancy; SAET may apply the minimum standard for severance payments as it thinks fit, and need not make an order (s 72B(5)-(7))`
    `yes, in time: you applied within 21 days after your employment was terminated for redundancy; SAET may apply the minimum standard for severance payments as it thinks fit, and need not make an order (s 72B(5)-(7))`
    `not covered: s 72B(5) names only an employee, a group of employees, or a registered association acting for them`
    `no: an application may be made only after notice of a pending redundancy has been given or the employment has been terminated for redundancy (s 72B(6)(a))`
    `no: the application was not made within 21 days after the notice was given or the employment was terminated (s 72B(6)(b))`

GIVEN `the event day` IS A DATE, `applied on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `safw within 21 days after (s 72B(6)(b))` `the event day` `applied on` IF
        (NOT `safw the day` `applied on` `is before` `the event day`)
    AND `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 72B(6)(b) -- the application must be made within 21 days after the notice or the termination` 21) `I, the claimant` `apply for a severance payment order (s 72B(6)(b))` (`safw days from` `the event day` `to` `applied on`)

GIVEN `the applicant` IS A `Severance applicant`,
      `notice of pending redundancy given` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice given on` IS A DATE,
      `terminated for redundancy` IS A BOOLEAN, `terminated on` IS A DATE,
      `applied on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Severance answer`
`safw can I apply for a severance order (s 72B(5), (6))` `the applicant` `notice of pending redundancy given` `notice given on` `terminated for redundancy` `terminated on` `applied on` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the applicant` EQUALS `someone else applying` THEN `not covered: s 72B(5) names only an employee, a group of employees, or a registered association acting for them`
           IF (NOT (`notice of pending redundancy given` OR `terminated for redundancy`)) THEN `no: an application may be made only after notice of a pending redundancy has been given or the employment has been terminated for redundancy (s 72B(6)(a))`
           IF `notice of pending redundancy given` AND `safw within 21 days after (s 72B(6)(b))` `notice given on` `applied on` THEN `yes, in time: you applied within 21 days after you were given notice of the pending redundancy; SAET may apply the minimum standard for severance payments as it thinks fit, and need not make an order (s 72B(5)-(7))`
           IF `terminated for redundancy` AND `safw within 21 days after (s 72B(6)(b))` `terminated on` `applied on` THEN `yes, in time: you applied within 21 days after your employment was terminated for redundancy; SAET may apply the minimum standard for severance payments as it thinks fit, and need not make an order (s 72B(5)-(7))`
           OTHERWISE `no: the application was not made within 21 days after the notice was given or the employment was terminated (s 72B(6)(b))`

§§ `Chapter 3 Part 3A Division 3: an outworker's unpaid remuneration claim (ss 99B, 99D-99G)`

-- s 99D(6): work performed after the commencement of s 99D (16 May 2005, Industrial Law Reform (Fair Work) Act 2005,
-- 3/2005 s 46, per the legislative history). Work on the commencement day is read as after it (fork F7).
`safw s 99D commenced on` MEANS `safw date` 16 5 2005

GIVEN `work completed on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`safw the last day to serve an unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(3))` `work completed on` MEANS `safw the last day of` 6 `months after` `work completed on`

DECLARE `Outworker claim answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your unpaid remuneration claim is validly made and in time; the apparent responsible contractor is liable for the amount claimed, unless within 14 days it refers the claim to your employer and the employer accepts liability by paying you (ss 99D, 99E, 99F)`
    `no: only an outworker may make an unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(1))`
    `no: as an outworker you can use Part 3A only if an award or enterprise agreement provision relates to outworkers or a regulation extends the Act to them; outside Chapter 1 the Act applies to outworkers only then (s 5(4))`
    `no: a person whose sole business in the clothing industry is retail sale is not a responsible contractor (s 99B(3))`
    `not covered: s 99D(1) lets you claim only against a person you believe on reasonable grounds to be a responsible contractor for you, one who initiated an order for the work (other than as a retail purchaser) or distributed it (s 99B(1))`
    `no: a claim may only be made for work performed after s 99D commenced on 16 May 2005 (s 99D(6))`
    `no: the claim was not made within 6 months after the work was completed (s 99D(3))`
    `not yet made: the claim is made by serving a written notice that claims payment and sets out your name, your contact address, a description of the work, the dates it was done and the amount claimed, with the particulars verified by statutory declaration (s 99D(4), (5))`

GIVEN `an outworker` IS A BOOLEAN, `an award, agreement or regulation brings outworkers in` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable belief the person initiated or distributed the work` IS A BOOLEAN, `the person's sole clothing business is retail sale` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `work performed on` IS A DATE, `work completed on` IS A DATE, `notice served on` IS A DATE,
      `notice claims payment` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice gives my name` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice gives my contact address` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `notice describes the work` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice gives the dates of the work` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice gives the amount claimed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `particulars verified by statutory declaration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Outworker claim answer`
`safw can I make an unpaid remuneration claim (ss 99B, 99D)` `an outworker` `an award, agreement or regulation brings outworkers in` `reasonable belief the person initiated or distributed the work` `the person's sole clothing business is retail sale` `work performed on` `work completed on` `notice served on` `notice claims payment` `notice gives my name` `notice gives my contact address` `notice describes the work` `notice gives the dates of the work` `notice gives the amount claimed` `particulars verified by statutory declaration` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `an outworker`) THEN `no: only an outworker may make an unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(1))`
           IF (NOT `an award, agreement or regulation brings outworkers in`) THEN `no: as an outworker you can use Part 3A only if an award or enterprise agreement provision relates to outworkers or a regulation extends the Act to them; outside Chapter 1 the Act applies to outworkers only then (s 5(4))`
           IF `the person's sole clothing business is retail sale` THEN `no: a person whose sole business in the clothing industry is retail sale is not a responsible contractor (s 99B(3))`
           IF (NOT `reasonable belief the person initiated or distributed the work`) THEN `not covered: s 99D(1) lets you claim only against a person you believe on reasonable grounds to be a responsible contractor for you, one who initiated an order for the work (other than as a retail purchaser) or distributed it (s 99B(1))`
           IF `safw the day` `work performed on` `is before` `safw s 99D commenced on` THEN `no: a claim may only be made for work performed after s 99D commenced on 16 May 2005 (s 99D(6))`
           IF (NOT `served in time`) THEN `no: the claim was not made within 6 months after the work was completed (s 99D(3))`
           IF (NOT `the notice is complete`) THEN `not yet made: the claim is made by serving a written notice that claims payment and sets out your name, your contact address, a description of the work, the dates it was done and the amount claimed, with the particulars verified by statutory declaration (s 99D(4), (5))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: your unpaid remuneration claim is validly made and in time; the apparent responsible contractor is liable for the amount claimed, unless within 14 days it refers the claim to your employer and the employer accepts liability by paying you (ss 99D, 99E, 99F)`
    WHERE
        `served in time` MEANS
            IF `safw the day` `notice served on` `is before` `work completed on`
            THEN TRUE
            ELSE `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 99D(3) -- the unpaid remuneration claim must be made within 6 months after the work is completed` (`safw days from` `work completed on` `to` (`safw the last day to serve an unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(3))` `work completed on`))) `I, the claimant` `serve the unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(3))` (`safw days from` `work completed on` `to` `notice served on`)
        `the notice is complete` MEANS
                `notice claims payment` AND `notice gives my name` AND `notice gives my contact address`
            AND `notice describes the work` AND `notice gives the dates of the work` AND `notice gives the amount claimed`
            AND `particulars verified by statutory declaration`

-- ss 99E, 99F: what the apparent responsible contractor remains liable for. The designated employer's acceptance
-- relieves the contractor only if the contractor referred the claim within 14 days after being served (s 99E(2), (4))
-- and the employer paid within 14 days after being served with the referred claim (s 99F(1)), and only to the extent
-- paid (up to the amount claimed).
GIVEN `amount claimed` IS A NUMBER,
      `the contractor referred the claim` IS A BOOLEAN, `days after service the contractor referred it` IS A NUMBER,
      `amount the designated employer paid me` IS A NUMBER, `days after the referred claim was served that the employer paid` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw the amount the apparent responsible contractor is liable for (ss 99E, 99F)` `amount claimed` `the contractor referred the claim` `days after service the contractor referred it` `amount the designated employer paid me` `days after the referred claim was served that the employer paid` MEANS
    IF `the referral counts` AND `the payment counts`
    THEN `amount claimed` MINUS `the part accepted`
    ELSE `amount claimed`
    WHERE
        `the referral counts` MEANS `the contractor referred the claim` AND `days after service the contractor referred it` AT MOST 14
        `the payment counts` MEANS `amount the designated employer paid me` GREATER THAN 0 AND `days after the referred claim was served that the employer paid` AT MOST 14
        `the part accepted` MEANS
            IF `amount the designated employer paid me` GREATER THAN `amount claimed`
            THEN `amount claimed`
            ELSE `amount the designated employer paid me`

DECLARE `Recovery answer` IS ONE OF
    `the Court must order the apparent responsible contractor to pay the amount claimed (s 99G(1), (3))`
    `no order is required: the apparent responsible contractor has satisfied the Court that the work was not performed (s 99G(3))`
    `no order for the amount claimed is required: the apparent responsible contractor has satisfied the Court that it is not the correct amount for the work (s 99G(3))`
    `nothing to recover: nothing remains payable by the apparent responsible contractor (s 99G(1))`

GIVEN `amount still payable by the contractor` IS A NUMBER, `contractor proves the work was not performed` IS A BOOLEAN, `contractor proves the amount is not correct` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Recovery answer`
`safw must the Court order the contractor to pay (s 99G)` `amount still payable by the contractor` `contractor proves the work was not performed` `contractor proves the amount is not correct` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `amount still payable by the contractor` AT MOST 0 THEN `nothing to recover: nothing remains payable by the apparent responsible contractor (s 99G(1))`
           IF `contractor proves the work was not performed` THEN `no order is required: the apparent responsible contractor has satisfied the Court that the work was not performed (s 99G(3))`
           IF `contractor proves the amount is not correct` THEN `no order for the amount claimed is required: the apparent responsible contractor has satisfied the Court that it is not the correct amount for the work (s 99G(3))`
           OTHERWISE `the Court must order the apparent responsible contractor to pay the amount claimed (s 99G(1), (3))`

§§ `Chapter 3 Part 5 Division 2: civil penalties for unpaid entitlements (ss 104-104C)`

-- Fair Work (Worker Entitlements) Amendment Act 2025, Sch 1 cl 4: its amendments (which inserted this Division) apply
-- only to conduct occurring after it commenced on 1 January 2026. Conduct on that day is read as after (fork F8).
`safw the 2025 amendments commenced on` MEANS `safw date` 1 1 2026

`safw s 104(1) maximum civil penalty` MEANS 25000

GIVEN `contravention on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`safw the last day to commence civil penalty proceedings (s 104A(9))` `contravention on` MEANS `safw the last day of` 6 `years after` `contravention on`

DECLARE `Civil penalty applicant` IS ONE OF
    `I am an employee affected by the contravention`
    `a registered association entitled to represent my industrial interests`
    `an inspector`
    `someone else applying for a penalty`

DECLARE `Civil penalty application answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the application may be made and is in time; the Court may order a civil penalty, part or all of it payable to a particular person, and recoverable as a debt (s 104A(2), (6), (7), (8), (9))`
    `not covered: s 104A(6) names only an affected employee, a registered association entitled to represent the employee's industrial interests, or an inspector`
    `no: the contravention happened before 1 January 2026, and the Division applies only to conduct after the 2025 amendments commenced (Fair Work (Worker Entitlements) Amendment Act 2025, Sch 1 cl 4)`
    `no: civil penalty proceedings may be commenced only within 6 years after the day the contravention occurred (s 104A(9))`
    `no: an order has already been made against the employer under a civil penalty provision of a State or Commonwealth Act for the same or substantially the same conduct, so the Court must not make an order (s 104B(3))`

GIVEN `the applicant` IS A `Civil penalty applicant`, `contravention on` IS A DATE, `application on` IS A DATE, `an earlier civil penalty order for the same conduct` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Civil penalty application answer`
`safw can a civil penalty application be made (s 104A)` `the applicant` `contravention on` `application on` `an earlier civil penalty order for the same conduct` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the applicant` EQUALS `someone else applying for a penalty` THEN `not covered: s 104A(6) names only an affected employee, a registered association entitled to represent the employee's industrial interests, or an inspector`
           IF `safw the day` `contravention on` `is before` `safw the 2025 amendments commenced on` THEN `no: the contravention happened before 1 January 2026, and the Division applies only to conduct after the 2025 amendments commenced (Fair Work (Worker Entitlements) Amendment Act 2025, Sch 1 cl 4)`
           IF (NOT `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 104A(9) -- civil penalty proceedings may be commenced within 6 years after the contravention` (`safw days from` `contravention on` `to` (`safw the last day to commence civil penalty proceedings (s 104A(9))` `contravention on`))) `I, the claimant` `commence civil penalty proceedings (s 104A(9))` (`safw days from` `contravention on` `to` `application on`)) THEN `no: civil penalty proceedings may be commenced only within 6 years after the day the contravention occurred (s 104A(9))`
           IF `an earlier civil penalty order for the same conduct` THEN `no: an order has already been made against the employer under a civil penalty provision of a State or Commonwealth Act for the same or substantially the same conduct, so the Court must not make an order (s 104B(3))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the application may be made and is in time; the Court may order a civil penalty, part or all of it payable to a particular person, and recoverable as a debt (s 104A(2), (6), (7), (8), (9))`

-- s 104(4), (5): deliberate, and part of a systematic pattern.
GIVEN `the conduct was deliberate` IS A BOOLEAN, `the employer is the Crown or a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN, `it authorised the conduct` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `safw the conduct was deliberate (s 104(4)(a), (5))` `the conduct was deliberate` `the employer is the Crown or a body corporate` `it authorised the conduct` IF
    `the conduct was deliberate` OR (`the employer is the Crown or a body corporate` AND `it authorised the conduct`)

DECLARE `Civil penalty answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Court may order a civil penalty of up to $25 000 for each contravention, counting contraventions arising out of one course of conduct as one (s 104(1), (4), s 104A(2)-(5))`
    `no: the Court is not satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the employer contravened s 104(1) (s 104A(2))`
    `no: a civil penalty may be imposed only if the contravention was deliberate (s 104(4)(a))`
    `no: a civil penalty may be imposed only if the contravention was part of a systematic pattern of conduct relating to one or more employees (s 104(4)(b))`

GIVEN `the Court is satisfied of the contravention` IS A BOOLEAN, `deliberate` IS A BOOLEAN, `systematic pattern` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Civil penalty answer`
`safw can a civil penalty be imposed (s 104(4), s 104A(2))` `the Court is satisfied of the contravention` `deliberate` `systematic pattern` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the Court is satisfied of the contravention`) THEN `no: the Court is not satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the employer contravened s 104(1) (s 104A(2))`
           IF (NOT `deliberate`) THEN `no: a civil penalty may be imposed only if the contravention was deliberate (s 104(4)(a))`
           IF (NOT `systematic pattern`) THEN `no: a civil penalty may be imposed only if the contravention was part of a systematic pattern of conduct relating to one or more employees (s 104(4)(b))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the Court may order a civil penalty of up to $25 000 for each contravention, counting contraventions arising out of one course of conduct as one (s 104(1), (4), s 104A(2)-(5))`

-- s 104A(4), (5): contraventions arising out of one course of conduct count as one, except those committed after the
-- Court has imposed a civil penalty for an earlier contravention, which count separately.
GIVEN `contraventions` IS A NUMBER, `committed after an earlier civil penalty` IS A NUMBER, `one course of conduct` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw contraventions counted (s 104A(4), (5))` `contraventions` `committed after an earlier civil penalty` `one course of conduct` MEANS
    IF `one course of conduct`
    THEN `the earlier ones counted` PLUS `the later ones`
    ELSE `contraventions`
    WHERE
        `the later ones` MEANS
            IF `committed after an earlier civil penalty` GREATER THAN `contraventions`
            THEN `contraventions`
            ELSE `committed after an earlier civil penalty`
        `the earlier ones counted` MEANS
            IF `contraventions` MINUS `the later ones` GREATER THAN 0
            THEN 1
            ELSE 0

GIVEN `the answer` IS A `Civil penalty answer`, `counted` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw the most the civil penalty can be (s 104(1), s 104A(3))` `the answer` `counted` MEANS
    IF `the answer` EQUALS `yes: the Court may order a civil penalty of up to $25 000 for each contravention, counting contraventions arising out of one course of conduct as one (s 104(1), (4), s 104A(2)-(5))`
    THEN `safw s 104(1) maximum civil penalty` TIMES `counted`
    ELSE 0

DECLARE `Civil penalty costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs, because the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 104C)`
    `no: a party to civil penalty proceedings may be ordered to pay another party's costs only if the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 104C)`

GIVEN `vexatiously or without reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN, `unreasonable act or omission caused the costs` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Civil penalty costs answer`
`safw costs in civil penalty proceedings (s 104C)` `vexatiously or without reasonable cause` `unreasonable act or omission caused the costs` MEANS
    IF `safw a ground for costs (s 36(2), s 104C)` `vexatiously or without reasonable cause` `unreasonable act or omission caused the costs`
    THEN `possibly: a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs, because the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 104C)`
    ELSE `no: a party to civil penalty proceedings may be ordered to pay another party's costs only if the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 104C)`

§§ `Chapter 3 Part 6: unfair dismissal (ss 105-111)`

DECLARE `Part 6 answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: Part 6 (unfair dismissal) applies to your dismissal`
    `no: Part 6 applies only to an employee under this Act whose employment the Act covers (ss 4, 6)`
    `no: Part 6 does not apply to an apprentice; an apprentice may apply to SAET under the South Australian Skills Act 2008 (s 105A(1)(b))`
    `no: Part 6 does not apply to a non-award employee whose remuneration immediately before the dismissal was at or above the indexed threshold a year (s 105A(1)(a))`
    `no: Part 6 does not apply to the end of a contract for a specified period or task at its end, unless the employer's conduct gave you a clear expectation of continuing employment (s 105A(4))`
    `no: the regulations exclude you from Part 6 (s 105A(2))`

GIVEN `the Act applies to my employment` IS A BOOLEAN, `an apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN, `covered by an industrial instrument` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `remuneration a year immediately before the dismissal` IS A NUMBER, `the indexed threshold in force` IS A NUMBER,
      `a specified period or task contract ended at its end` IS A BOOLEAN, `a clear expectation of continuing employment from the employer's conduct` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `excluded by regulation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Part 6 answer`
`safw does Part 6 apply to my dismissal (s 105A)` `the Act applies to my employment` `an apprentice` `covered by an industrial instrument` `remuneration a year immediately before the dismissal` `the indexed threshold in force` `a specified period or task contract ended at its end` `a clear expectation of continuing employment from the employer's conduct` `excluded by regulation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the Act applies to my employment`) THEN `no: Part 6 applies only to an employee under this Act whose employment the Act covers (ss 4, 6)`
           IF `an apprentice` THEN `no: Part 6 does not apply to an apprentice; an apprentice may apply to SAET under the South Australian Skills Act 2008 (s 105A(1)(b))`
           IF (NOT `covered by an industrial instrument`) AND `remuneration a year immediately before the dismissal` AT LEAST `the indexed threshold in force` THEN `no: Part 6 does not apply to a non-award employee whose remuneration immediately before the dismissal was at or above the indexed threshold a year (s 105A(1)(a))`
           IF `a specified period or task contract ended at its end` AND (NOT `a clear expectation of continuing employment from the employer's conduct`) THEN `no: Part 6 does not apply to the end of a contract for a specified period or task at its end, unless the employer's conduct gave you a clear expectation of continuing employment (s 105A(4))`
           IF `excluded by regulation` THEN `no: the regulations exclude you from Part 6 (s 105A(2))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: Part 6 (unfair dismissal) applies to your dismissal`

-- s 106(1): "before the end of a period of 21 days from the date the dismissal takes effect"; the period excludes the
-- day the dismissal takes effect, so it ends at the end of the 21st day after it (fork F1).
GIVEN `dismissal took effect on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`safw the last day to apply for unfair dismissal relief (s 106(1))` `dismissal took effect on` MEANS `safw the date` 21 `days after` `dismissal took effect on`

DECLARE `Unfair dismissal time answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you applied before the end of 21 days from the date the dismissal took effect; no fee may be charged (s 106(1), (4))`
    `in time: the 21 days do not apply, because the proceedings were referred to SAET under another law (s 106(3a))`
    `too early: the dismissal had not taken effect when you applied (s 106(1))`
    `no: you cannot bring proceedings for the dismissal before two or more adjudicating authorities at once (s 106(2))`
    `out of time: you did not apply before the end of 21 days from the date the dismissal took effect (s 106(1)); Part 6 gives no power to extend the time (whether the South Australian Employment Tribunal Act 2014 does is outside this encoding)`

GIVEN `dismissal took effect on` IS A DATE, `applied on` IS A DATE, `referred to SAET under another law` IS A BOOLEAN, `proceedings for the dismissal on foot before another adjudicating authority` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Unfair dismissal time answer`
`safw is my unfair dismissal application in time (s 106)` `dismissal took effect on` `applied on` `referred to SAET under another law` `proceedings for the dismissal on foot before another adjudicating authority` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `proceedings for the dismissal on foot before another adjudicating authority` THEN `no: you cannot bring proceedings for the dismissal before two or more adjudicating authorities at once (s 106(2))`
           IF `referred to SAET under another law` THEN `in time: the 21 days do not apply, because the proceedings were referred to SAET under another law (s 106(3a))`
           IF `safw the day` `applied on` `is before` `dismissal took effect on` THEN `too early: the dismissal had not taken effect when you applied (s 106(1))`
           IF `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 106(1) -- the employee applies before the end of 21 days from the date the dismissal takes effect` (`safw days from` `dismissal took effect on` `to` (`safw the last day to apply for unfair dismissal relief (s 106(1))` `dismissal took effect on`))) `I, the claimant` `apply to SAET for unfair dismissal relief (s 106(1))` (`safw days from` `dismissal took effect on` `to` `applied on`) THEN `in time: you applied before the end of 21 days from the date the dismissal took effect; no fee may be charged (s 106(1), (4))`
           OTHERWISE `out of time: you did not apply before the end of 21 days from the date the dismissal took effect (s 106(1)); Part 6 gives no power to extend the time (whether the South Australian Employment Tribunal Act 2014 does is outside this encoding)`

DECLARE `Remedy answer` IS ONE OF
    `no relief: SAET is not satisfied that the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable (s 108(1), s 109(1))`
    `no relief: a dismissal with a redundancy payment under an industrial instrument cannot be harsh, unjust or unreasonable solely because the payment is inadequate (s 108(3))`
    `re-employment in your former position, without prejudice to your former conditions of employment, the preferred remedy (s 109(1)(a), (1a))`
    `re-employment by the employer in another available position, on conditions SAET determines (s 109(1)(b))`
    `compensation from the employer, in an amount SAET determines up to the s 109(3) cap (s 109(1)(c), (3))`
    `no remedy settled on these facts: re-employment is not ordered, yet SAET may award compensation only if satisfied that there are cogent reasons to believe re-employment would not be appropriate (s 109(1a)), unless the business has fewer than 50 employees (s 109(1b))`

GIVEN `SAET is satisfied the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the only ground is an inadequate redundancy payment under an industrial instrument` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `re-employment in my former position is impracticable or not appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `another position is available` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `SAET considers re-employment in any position not appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `SAET is satisfied of cogent reasons against re-employment` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `fewer than 50 employees in the business at the decision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Remedy answer`
`safw what can SAET order for my dismissal (ss 108, 109)` `SAET is satisfied the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable` `the only ground is an inadequate redundancy payment under an industrial instrument` `re-employment in my former position is impracticable or not appropriate` `another position is available` `SAET considers re-employment in any position not appropriate` `SAET is satisfied of cogent reasons against re-employment` `fewer than 50 employees in the business at the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the only ground is an inadequate redundancy payment under an industrial instrument` THEN `no relief: a dismissal with a redundancy payment under an industrial instrument cannot be harsh, unjust or unreasonable solely because the payment is inadequate (s 108(3))`
           IF (NOT `SAET is satisfied the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable`) THEN `no relief: SAET is not satisfied that the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable (s 108(1), s 109(1))`
           IF (NOT `re-employment in my former position is impracticable or not appropriate`) THEN `re-employment in your former position, without prejudice to your former conditions of employment, the preferred remedy (s 109(1)(a), (1a))`
           IF `another position is available` AND (NOT `SAET considers re-employment in any position not appropriate`) THEN `re-employment by the employer in another available position, on conditions SAET determines (s 109(1)(b))`
           IF `SAET considers re-employment in any position not appropriate` AND (`SAET is satisfied of cogent reasons against re-employment` OR `fewer than 50 employees in the business at the decision`) THEN `compensation from the employer, in an amount SAET determines up to the s 109(3) cap (s 109(1)(c), (3))`
           OTHERWISE `no remedy settled on these facts: re-employment is not ordered, yet SAET may award compensation only if satisfied that there are cogent reasons to believe re-employment would not be appropriate (s 109(1a)), unless the business has fewer than 50 employees (s 109(1b))`

-- s 109(3): the greater of 6 months' remuneration at the rate immediately before the dismissal, or the indexed figure.
GIVEN `remuneration a year immediately before the dismissal` IS A NUMBER, `the indexed figure in force` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw the compensation cap (s 109(3))` `remuneration a year immediately before the dismissal` `the indexed figure in force` MEANS
    IF `six months' remuneration` GREATER THAN `the indexed figure in force`
    THEN `six months' remuneration`
    ELSE `the indexed figure in force`
    WHERE
        `six months' remuneration` MEANS `remuneration a year immediately before the dismissal` DIVIDED BY 2

-- s 109(2)(a): back pay on re-employment, for the period from the date the dismissal took effect to the date of
-- re-employment; NOTHING where SAET has directed otherwise (the Act does not fix the amount then).
GIVEN `weekly remuneration` IS A NUMBER, `dismissal took effect on` IS A DATE, `re-employed on` IS A DATE, `SAET directed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`safw back pay on re-employment (s 109(2)(a))` `weekly remuneration` `dismissal took effect on` `re-employed on` `SAET directed otherwise` MEANS
    IF `SAET directed otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`weekly remuneration` TIMES (`safw days from` `dismissal took effect on` `to` `re-employed on`) DIVIDED BY 7)

DECLARE `Instalment answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: SAET may order payment by instalments (s 109(4))`
    `no: SAET may order instalments only if satisfied that exceptional circumstances justify it (s 109(4)(a))`
    `no: insofar as the order compensates lost remuneration, instalments are allowed only if at least as favourable to you as the remuneration would have been if the employment had continued (s 109(4)(b))`

GIVEN `SAET is satisfied of exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN, `the order compensates lost remuneration` IS A BOOLEAN, `the instalments are at least as favourable as the remuneration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Instalment answer`
`safw can my compensation be paid by instalments (s 109(4))` `SAET is satisfied of exceptional circumstances` `the order compensates lost remuneration` `the instalments are at least as favourable as the remuneration` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `SAET is satisfied of exceptional circumstances`) THEN `no: SAET may order instalments only if satisfied that exceptional circumstances justify it (s 109(4)(a))`
           IF `the order compensates lost remuneration` AND (NOT `the instalments are at least as favourable as the remuneration`) THEN `no: insofar as the order compensates lost remuneration, instalments are allowed only if at least as favourable to you as the remuneration would have been if the employment had continued (s 109(4)(b))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: SAET may order payment by instalments (s 109(4))`

-- s 110.
DECLARE `How the case ended` IS ONE OF `it went to a hearing`, `I discontinued it`, `it is still running`

DECLARE `Unfair dismissal costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the case went to hearing and SAET is satisfied you clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle before the hearing concluded, so SAET may order you to pay costs, including representation (s 110(1))`
    `possibly: you discontinued more than 14 days after the conference of the parties concluded and SAET is satisfied you acted unreasonably, so SAET may order you to pay the employer's costs, including representation (s 110(2))`
    `no: SAET may order costs against you only on the other party's application (s 110(1), (2))`
    `no: the application for costs was made more than 14 days after the determination or discontinuance (s 110(3))`
    `no: s 110 allows costs against you only if the case went to hearing and SAET is satisfied you clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle, or you discontinued more than 14 days after the conference and SAET is satisfied you acted unreasonably`

GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How the case ended`,
      `SAET satisfied I clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `days after the conference concluded that I discontinued` IS A NUMBER,
      `SAET satisfied I acted unreasonably in discontinuing late` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the other party applied for costs` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `days after the determination or discontinuance it applied` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN `Unfair dismissal costs answer`
`safw can I be ordered to pay costs in my unfair dismissal case (s 110)` `how it ended` `SAET satisfied I clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle` `days after the conference concluded that I discontinued` `SAET satisfied I acted unreasonably in discontinuing late` `the other party applied for costs` `days after the determination or discontinuance it applied` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `a ground`) THEN `no: s 110 allows costs against you only if the case went to hearing and SAET is satisfied you clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle, or you discontinued more than 14 days after the conference and SAET is satisfied you acted unreasonably`
           IF (NOT `the other party applied for costs`) THEN `no: SAET may order costs against you only on the other party's application (s 110(1), (2))`
           IF (NOT `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 110(3) -- an application for costs must be made within 14 days after the determination or discontinuance` 14) `the employer` `apply for costs (s 110(3))` `days after the determination or discontinuance it applied`) THEN `no: the application for costs was made more than 14 days after the determination or discontinuance (s 110(3))`
           IF `the hearing ground` THEN `possibly: the case went to hearing and SAET is satisfied you clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle before the hearing concluded, so SAET may order you to pay costs, including representation (s 110(1))`
           OTHERWISE `possibly: you discontinued more than 14 days after the conference of the parties concluded and SAET is satisfied you acted unreasonably, so SAET may order you to pay the employer's costs, including representation (s 110(2))`
    WHERE
        `the hearing ground` MEANS `how it ended` EQUALS `it went to a hearing` AND `SAET satisfied I clearly acted unreasonably in failing to discontinue or settle`
        `the discontinuance ground` MEANS
                `how it ended` EQUALS `I discontinued it`
            AND `days after the conference concluded that I discontinued` GREATER THAN 14
            AND `SAET satisfied I acted unreasonably in discontinuing late`
        `a ground` MEANS `the hearing ground` OR `the discontinuance ground`

-- s 111(1): SAET must hand down its determination and reasons within 3 months after final submissions.
GIVEN `final submissions finished on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`safw the last day for SAET's determination (s 111(1))` `final submissions finished on` MEANS `safw the last day of` 3 `months after` `final submissions finished on`

§§ `Schedule 8: rules for terminating employment`

-- Sch 8 cl 1(2). Continuous service runs from the day employment began to the day it is terminated; it is "more than
-- N years" when the termination day is after the N-th anniversary of the start, and "at least 2 years" have been
-- completed when the termination day is on or after the 2nd anniversary. The employee is "over 45 years old" when the
-- termination day is after the 45th birthday (fork F9). No event is disregarded (the regulations under cl 1(3) are not
-- deposited).
GIVEN `service began on` IS A DATE, `terminated on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw base weeks of notice (Sch 8 cl 1(2)(a)-(d))` `service began on` `terminated on` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `more than` 5 THEN 4
           IF `more than` 3 THEN 3
           IF `more than` 1 THEN 2
           OTHERWISE 1
    WHERE
        GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
        GIVETH A BOOLEAN
        `more than` n MEANS `safw the day` (`safw the last day of` n `years after` `service began on`) `is before` `terminated on`

GIVEN `service began on` IS A DATE, `terminated on` IS A DATE, `born on` IS A DATE, `serious misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`safw weeks of notice (Sch 8 cl 1)` `service began on` `terminated on` `born on` `serious misconduct` MEANS
    IF `serious misconduct`
    THEN 0
    ELSE `safw base weeks of notice (Sch 8 cl 1(2)(a)-(d))` `service began on` `terminated on` PLUS `the extra week`
    WHERE
        `over 45` MEANS `safw the day` (`safw the last day of` 45 `years after` `born on`) `is before` `terminated on`
        `two years completed` MEANS (NOT `safw the day` `terminated on` `is before` (`safw the last day of` 2 `years after` `service began on`))
        `the extra week` MEANS
            IF `over 45` AND `two years completed`
            THEN 1
            ELSE 0

DECLARE `Respond answer` IS ONE OF
    `Sch 8 cl 2 does not apply: the termination was not for reasons related to your conduct or performance`
    `the rule was kept: you were given the opportunity to defend yourself against the allegations (Sch 8 cl 2(a))`
    `the rule was kept: the employer could not reasonably be expected to give you that opportunity (Sch 8 cl 2(b))`
    `no: the employer must not terminate your employment for reasons related to your conduct or performance without giving you the opportunity to defend yourself (Sch 8 cl 2); SAET must have regard to this in deciding whether the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable (s 108(2)(b))`

GIVEN `for conduct or performance` IS A BOOLEAN, `given the opportunity to defend myself` IS A BOOLEAN, `the employer could not reasonably be expected to give it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Respond answer`
`safw was I given the chance to respond (Sch 8 cl 2)` `for conduct or performance` `given the opportunity to defend myself` `the employer could not reasonably be expected to give it` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `for conduct or performance`) THEN `Sch 8 cl 2 does not apply: the termination was not for reasons related to your conduct or performance`
           IF `given the opportunity to defend myself` THEN `the rule was kept: you were given the opportunity to defend yourself against the allegations (Sch 8 cl 2(a))`
           IF `the employer could not reasonably be expected to give it` THEN `the rule was kept: the employer could not reasonably be expected to give you that opportunity (Sch 8 cl 2(b))`
           OTHERWISE `no: the employer must not terminate your employment for reasons related to your conduct or performance without giving you the opportunity to defend yourself (Sch 8 cl 2); SAET must have regard to this in deciding whether the dismissal was harsh, unjust or unreasonable (s 108(2)(b))`

§§ `Prohibited reasons, industrial proceedings, and after a conviction (ss 115, 116B, 223, 233, 235(2))`

`safw s 116B and s 223 maximum penalty` MEANS 20000

DECLARE `Employer act` IS ONE OF
    `dismissed me, or threatened to`
    `injured me in my employment, or threatened to`
    `altered my position to my prejudice, or threatened to`
    `refused to employ me, or threatened to`
    `discriminated against me in the terms or conditions on which it offered to employ me, or threatened to`
    `something else the employer did`

DECLARE `Prohibited reason` IS ONE OF
    `(a) I am, have been or propose to become an officer, delegate or member of an association`
    `(b) I am not, or do not propose to become, a member of an association`
    `(c) I have employees who are not association members, or have not paid an association fee`
    `(d) I refused or failed to join in industrial action`
    `(e) I refused to agree to or vote for an agreement my association would be party to`
    `(f) I applied or proposed to apply to an industrial authority for a secret ballot order`
    `(g) I took part or proposed to take part in a secret ballot ordered by an industrial authority`
    `(h) I am entitled to the benefit of an instrument on conditions of employment or an industrial authority's order`
    `(i) I made or proposed an inquiry or complaint to a body able to seek compliance with industrial law or enforcement of my rights`
    `(j) I took part or proposed to take part in a proceeding under an industrial relations law`
    `(k) I gave or proposed to give evidence in a proceeding under an industrial relations law`
    `(l) an association is seeking better industrial conditions for me`
    `(m) I was absent from work without leave`
    `(n) I did or proposed a lawful act as an association officer or member to further or protect its industrial interests`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Prohibited reason answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is an offence for an employer to do this for a prohibited reason, maximum penalty $20 000 (s 115, s 116B(1)); once the employer is convicted you may apply to that court for compensation for your loss and, if you were dismissed, re-employment (s 116B(2)); a prosecution must be commenced within 2 years (s 235(2))`
    `not covered: s 116B(1) lists only dismissal, injury in employment, prejudicial alteration of position, refusal to employ and discriminatory terms of an offer, done or threatened`
    `no: the employer did not act for a prohibited reason; the reasons are listed in s 115`
    `no: absence without leave is a prohibited reason only if the absence was to carry out duties or exercise rights as an officer of an association, and you applied for leave beforehand and it was unreasonably refused or withheld (s 115(m))`

GIVEN `what the employer did` IS AN `Employer act`, `the reason` IS A `Prohibited reason`,
      `absent for duties or rights as an association officer` IS A BOOLEAN, `applied for leave beforehand and it was unreasonably refused or withheld` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Prohibited reason answer`
`safw was it done for a prohibited reason (ss 115, 116B)` `what the employer did` `the reason` `absent for duties or rights as an association officer` `applied for leave beforehand and it was unreasonably refused or withheld` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `what the employer did` EQUALS `something else the employer did` THEN `not covered: s 116B(1) lists only dismissal, injury in employment, prejudicial alteration of position, refusal to employ and discriminatory terms of an offer, done or threatened`
           IF `the reason` EQUALS `none of these` THEN `no: the employer did not act for a prohibited reason; the reasons are listed in s 115`
           IF `the reason` EQUALS `(m) I was absent from work without leave` AND (NOT (`absent for duties or rights as an association officer` AND `applied for leave beforehand and it was unreasonably refused or withheld`)) THEN `no: absence without leave is a prohibited reason only if the absence was to carry out duties or exercise rights as an officer of an association, and you applied for leave beforehand and it was unreasonably refused or withheld (s 115(m))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: it is an offence for an employer to do this for a prohibited reason, maximum penalty $20 000 (s 115, s 116B(1)); once the employer is convicted you may apply to that court for compensation for your loss and, if you were dismissed, re-employment (s 116B(2)); a prosecution must be commenced within 2 years (s 235(2))`

DECLARE `Proceedings act` IS ONE OF
    `dismissed me`
    `threatened to dismiss me`
    `prejudiced me in my employment`
    `threatened to prejudice me in my employment`
    `something else done to me`

DECLARE `Proceedings reason` IS ONE OF
    `(a) my participation in proceedings before SAET`
    `(b) something I said or did, or did not say or do, in proceedings before SAET`
    `(c) my participation in an industrial dispute`
    `(d) I am entitled to the benefit of an award or enterprise agreement, or took part or declined to take part in negotiations or proceedings for one`
    `another reason`

DECLARE `Proceedings answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is an offence (s 223(1)), maximum penalty $20 000; you may commence the prosecution yourself (s 223(3)) within 2 years (s 235(2)); once the employer is convicted you may apply for compensation for your loss and, if you were dismissed, re-employment (s 223(4))`
    `no: discrimination on the ground that you contravened a determination of SAET or committed an offence is not made unlawful by s 223(1) (s 223(2))`
    `not covered: s 223(1) reaches only dismissal or prejudice in employment, done or threatened`
    `not covered: s 223(1) reaches only the reasons it lists (SAET proceedings, an industrial dispute, an award or enterprise agreement)`

GIVEN `what the employer did` IS A `Proceedings act`, `the reason` IS A `Proceedings reason`, `the ground was my contravention of a SAET determination or an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Proceedings answer`
`safw was it done because I took part in industrial proceedings (s 223)` `what the employer did` `the reason` `the ground was my contravention of a SAET determination or an offence` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `what the employer did` EQUALS `something else done to me` THEN `not covered: s 223(1) reaches only dismissal or prejudice in employment, done or threatened`
           IF `the ground was my contravention of a SAET determination or an offence` THEN `no: discrimination on the ground that you contravened a determination of SAET or committed an offence is not made unlawful by s 223(1) (s 223(2))`
           IF `the reason` EQUALS `another reason` THEN `not covered: s 223(1) reaches only the reasons it lists (SAET proceedings, an industrial dispute, an award or enterprise agreement)`
           OTHERWISE `yes: it is an offence (s 223(1)), maximum penalty $20 000; you may commence the prosecution yourself (s 223(3)) within 2 years (s 235(2)); once the employer is convicted you may apply for compensation for your loss and, if you were dismissed, re-employment (s 223(4))`

-- s 235(2).
GIVEN `offence on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`safw the last day to prosecute (s 235(2))` `offence on` MEANS `safw the last day of` 2 `years after` `offence on`

DECLARE `Prosecution time answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: the prosecution was commenced within 2 years after the date of the alleged offence (s 235(2))`
    `out of time: a prosecution must be commenced within 2 years after the date of the alleged offence (s 235(2))`
    `too early: the alleged offence date is after the date the prosecution was commenced`

GIVEN `offence on` IS A DATE, `commenced on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Prosecution time answer`
`safw is the prosecution in time (s 235(2))` `offence on` `commenced on` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `safw the day` `commenced on` `is before` `offence on` THEN `too early: the alleged offence date is after the date the prosecution was commenced`
           IF `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 235(2) -- a prosecution must be commenced within 2 years after the offence` (`safw days from` `offence on` `to` (`safw the last day to prosecute (s 235(2))` `offence on`))) `the prosecutor` `commence the prosecution (s 235(2))` (`safw days from` `offence on` `to` `commenced on`) THEN `in time: the prosecution was commenced within 2 years after the date of the alleged offence (s 235(2))`
           OTHERWISE `out of time: a prosecution must be commenced within 2 years after the date of the alleged offence (s 235(2))`

DECLARE `Conviction offence` IS ONE OF
    `s 116B, dismissal or other action for a prohibited reason`
    `s 223, discrimination for taking part in industrial proceedings`
    `another offence against this Act committed in respect of me`

DECLARE `After conviction answer` IS ONE OF
    `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence and an order that the employer re-employ you on conditions the court determines (s 116B(2)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
    `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence (s 116B(2)(a)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
    `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence and an order that the employer re-employ you on conditions the court determines (s 223(4)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
    `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence (s 223(4)(a)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
    `you may apply to the convicting court for an order that the employer pay you an amount it owes you; an inspector's certificate proves the amount unless the contrary is proved (s 233)`

GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Conviction offence`, `I was dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `After conviction answer`
`safw what can I apply for after the conviction (ss 116B(2), 223(4), 233)` `the offence` `I was dismissed` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `s 116B, dismissal or other action for a prohibited reason` THEN
        IF `I was dismissed`
        THEN `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence and an order that the employer re-employ you on conditions the court determines (s 116B(2)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
        ELSE `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence (s 116B(2)(a)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
    WHEN `s 223, discrimination for taking part in industrial proceedings` THEN
        IF `I was dismissed`
        THEN `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence and an order that the employer re-employ you on conditions the court determines (s 223(4)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
        ELSE `you may apply to the convicting court for compensation for loss resulting from the offence (s 223(4)(a)); and for an order to pay any amount the employer owes you (s 233)`
    OTHERWISE `you may apply to the convicting court for an order that the employer pay you an amount it owes you; an inspector's certificate proves the amount unless the contrary is proved (s 233)`
